+# Decision: Self-hosted WebSockets for change delivery
+
+## Context
+
+MarketBoard pushes committed board changes to connected staff browsers. The freshness goal is 15 seconds under normal connectivity; subsecond updates would be useful but are not required. The board runs up to 800 simultaneous browsers with bursts of 20 committed edits per second at market opening. Three delivery routes can meet the freshness and scale numbers, so the choice turns on cost, operational burden, and coupling — not on capability. This decision builds on **Commands over HTTPS, one authoritative board per event** (HLD Key Design Decisions): delivery distributes already-committed revisions and never creates them, so any route must respect the one-authoritative-revision tenet.
+
+Getting this wrong is expensive to reverse: the delivery route shapes the change store's write path, the delivery component's entire shape, and the browser client's sync model. Switching later means reworking all three.
+
+## Decision Elements
+
+- **Vendor coupling** *(major)*. How much of the delivery path depends on a third party's proprietary protocol, SDK, and pricing. A route that couples the board to a realtime vendor makes the board's core loop hostage to that vendor's availability and fee schedule.
+- **Recurring cost at scale** *(moderate)*. 800 concurrent connections during event hours is the delivery path's steady state. Per-connection or per-message fees compound with market growth; self-hosted costs scale with server capacity instead.
+- **Operational burden the team owns** *(moderate)*. Connection capacity planning, session lifecycle, dead-connection pruning, and reconnect handling are real work. The team is small and the pilot is single-region.
+- **Update latency** *(moderate)*. The goal is 15 seconds; subsecond is useful, not required. A route that buys large latency improvements at large cost is overpaying for an unstated requirement.
+- **Read amplification on the change store** *(minor)*. Delivery routes differ in how often browsers re-read board state. Repeated polling reads are wasted work, but the store is small and the read cost is bounded.
+
+**Gates** — none of the candidate options fails a hard gate: all three meet the 15-second freshness goal and the 800-browser scale target. The selection below is made on the weighted criteria.
+
+## Options in the Domain
+
+### Five-second HTTPS polling
+
+**Description.** Every connected browser re-fetches the board view (or a delta since its last-seen revision cursor) on a fixed five-second timer. The server stays stateless per request: no connection registry, no session state. The change store gains a cursor-query index (revisions after revision N). Delivery is plain request/response over the existing HTTPS stack. Browsers run a polling loop, reset the timer on activity, and treat a failed poll as a connectivity signal.
+
+**Analysis.**
+- Vendor coupling — **strong**: everything is ordinary HTTP served by the team's own stack; no external dependency.
+- Recurring cost — **strong**: no per-connection fees; cost is ordinary request serving.
+- Operational burden — **strong**: the least new machinery of the three; stateless requests need no connection registry or reconnect protocol.
+- Update latency — **partial**: worst-case lag is roughly the poll interval plus processing; meets the 15-second goal with margin, but lags WebSockets by seconds on every change.
+- Read amplification — **weak**: 800 browsers polling every five seconds is ~160 requests/second steady state, most returning "nothing changed."
+
+**Summary.** Trades seconds of lag and steady redundant reads for the simplest, most stateless operation.
+
+### Self-hosted WebSockets *(chosen)*
+
+**Description.** The delivery component maintains WebSocket connections to browsers and pushes each committed event revision the moment it commits. The change store keeps a replayable revision history so a reconnecting browser can request the revisions it missed from the revision it last saw. Delivery owns the session layer: accepting connections, authenticating subscribers, pushing revisions, and pruning dead connections. Browsers run a reconnect loop — on disconnect, resubscribe and catch up from the last received revision rather than refetching the whole board. Capacity is sized for 800 concurrent connections with headroom for opening-time bursts.
+
+**Analysis.**
+- Vendor coupling — **strong**: the protocol is standard WebSocket over the team's own infrastructure; no realtime vendor sits in the loop.
+- Recurring cost — **strong**: infrastructure is ordinary server capacity, not per-connection metering.
+- Operational burden — **weak**: the team owns connection capacity planning, session lifecycle, reconnect semantics, and failure modes like half-open connections — the heaviest operations load of the three.
+- Update latency — **strong**: push-on-commit delivers changes in well under a second, comfortably inside the goal.
+- Read amplification — **strong**: revisions are pushed once per change, not re-requested per browser per interval; catch-up reads happen only after actual disconnects.
+
+**Summary.** Trades real, ongoing connection-operations work for fast updates and freedom from a realtime vendor.
+
+### Hosted realtime service
+
+**Description.** A managed realtime provider (pub/sub channels with a browser SDK) carries board updates. On commit, the server publishes the revision to the event's channel; the provider fans it out to subscribed browsers. The change store gains publication reconciliation — confirming that every committed revision was successfully published — because the provider is now a component that can fail between commit and delivery. The delivery component wraps the provider SDK behind the project's own boundary. Browsers use the provider's subscription model instead of a hand-rolled socket loop.
+
+**Analysis.**
+- Vendor coupling — **weak**: the board's core loop runs on the provider's proprietary SDK, protocol, and availability; migrating away later means reworking delivery and browser sync.
+- Recurring cost — **weak**: per-connection and per-message fees at 800 concurrent browsers recur every event weekend and scale with adoption.
+- Operational burden — **strong**: connection infrastructure and fan-out are the provider's problem; the team operates only the publish path.
+- Update latency — **strong**: push delivery comparable to self-hosted WebSockets.
+- Read amplification — **strong**: one publish per commit; browsers receive pushes like the WebSocket option.
+
+**Summary.** Trades recurring fees and provider coupling for the least connection operations work.
+
+## Selection
+
+**Self-hosted WebSockets.** All three options clear the hard requirements, so the decision turns on the weighted criteria. The hosted service is eliminated primarily by vendor coupling (major): the user weighed recurring fees (moderate) against it and judged tying the board's core delivery loop to a realtime vendor the worse long-term position for a pilot that may change shape. Polling is the honest runner-up — it wins on operational burden — but its wins are on criteria the goals do not demand: the freshness goal is 15 seconds, so polling's seconds of lag buy nothing the requirements ask for, while its steady redundant reads add cost without adding capability. Self-hosted WebSockets wins on latency (comfortable margin), read amplification, cost, and coupling, and loses only on operational burden (moderate) — a burden the user explicitly accepted in exchange for fast updates without vendor dependency.
+
+Committing obligates the downstream nodes: the change store must keep a replayable revision history with catch-up reads from a last-seen revision; delivery must own session lifecycle including dead-connection pruning; browser sync must own the reconnect-and-catch-up loop. It forecloses the hosted route's shape (provider wrapper, publication reconciliation) and the polling route's shape (cursor-poll endpoint, browser polling loop). This decision is taken *with* the tenet **Self-hosted and managed infrastructure are both permitted** — the tenet leaves the door open and the weighing above closes it on the self-hosted side.
