 ## Key Design Decisions
 
 - **Commands over HTTPS, one authoritative board per event.** Editors submit commands to the server, which assigns each committed edit one event revision. Peer-to-peer or browser-side merging was rejected: the tenets require one authoritative revision regardless of delivery route, and offline editing is a non-goal.
+- **Self-hosted WebSockets for change delivery.** Connected browsers receive pushed revisions over WebSocket connections the team operates. Five-second HTTPS polling was rejected as the default: it meets the freshness goal but multiplies snapshot reads and adds seconds of lag. A hosted realtime service was rejected to avoid recurring per-connection fees and provider coupling; the team accepts owning connection capacity, session lifecycle, and reconnect behavior instead. The store keeps a replayable revision history, delivery owns sessions, and browser sync owns reconnect/catch-up. Full reasoning: `docs/decisions/self-hosted-websockets-delivery.md`.
